  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$21.36 billion** of Chapter 12 goods in 2025, down **28.8%** from 2024. **Soybeans (heading 1201) are 77%** of that — almost the whole chapter — and the drop is almost entirely China: its purchases fell 74% in 2025 as Beijing bought more from Brazil and Argentina during the tariff fight, before U.S. exports of this chapter rebounded **25%** in the first seven months of 2026 on fresh Chinese buying.\n\nOutside China there is almost no tariff story here. Mexico, Canada, the EU, Japan and Egypt all buy this chapter's goods duty-free, so for most buyers it is harvest size, crushing demand and freight cost — not a tariff rate — that decides how much moves. China is the exception: on top of its 3% normal rate, it still charges the 10% tariff from the 2025 U.S.–China tariff fight, and its own September 2026 goodwill list left non-seed soybeans, this chapter's single biggest export, out of the tariff relief it gave other U.S. farm goods.",
